hi there it's Kevin Van Ord standing in  for reviewer Lafe Johnson to tell you  about the early access version of Daisy  you might think Daisy is about zombies  but in this survival simulation the  emphasis is not on the undead but on  your fellow humans that doesn't mean  that Dom bees don't pose a threat the  first time you spawn into the game you  might be slapped around by a brain eater  fresh out of the loading screen all the  while you'll fiddle with the menus just  trying to figure out how to pull out a  weapon and bash things with Daisy  doesn't just not hold your hand it chops  it right off and throws it to the greedy  undead in this frightening desolate  landscape your goal is simply to stay  alive by scavenging for food and weapons  finding proper shelter and fending off  anyone who threatens your survival if  you played ARMA 2 or the ARMA 2 mod that  spawned this standalone version of dayz  you'll make peace with the unusual  controls quickly and instead let the  perilous world envelop you know music  distracts you from the desolation and  every creak of nearby sheet metal sends  chills up your spine chancing upon a  town can be terrifying for all the  promise in those once cozy homes in  there picketed yards there's a chance  they'll deliver death that you never see  coming  dayz works so well as a survival sim  because it puts few barriers between you  and the world around you  gone are the tidy health and stamina  bars that sneak into the corners of  similar first-person games instead dayz  gnaws at your confidence with little  nags like my stomach grumbles and then  there are the communications you never  want to see such as I can feel warm  blood on my clothes dayz wisely reserves  its menus for essentials like its  inventory which sports a drag-and-drop  option in an improvement over the mod  the inventory itself expands greatly  once you discover pocketed items like  knapsacks and hoodies thus delivering  some of the satisfactions that discovery  of these items would yield in real life  it's the other elements that need work  such as the zombies themselves they  number so few that it's possible to go  half an hour without seeing one and when  you do there's a good chance you'll see  it running through walls or even  vanishing under the terrain zombies tend  to rush you from hundreds of yards away  if they see you and it usually takes a  little more than getting in their line  of sight to draw their attention no  matter how far you might be it's the  other players who instill the most fear  sometimes you'll come across a nice one  and a sense of camaraderie emerges as  you scrounge through buildings and take  out the living undead together most of  the time however others are out to kill  you some play nice at first and then  lead you into ambushes where unseen  snipers shoot you down still others may  trick you into coming near and then try  to bury an axe in your face because they  like the look of the hoodie you're  wearing attempting to hide from and  survive against humans with actual  intelligence elevates Daisy to great  heights of tension and unpredictability  he'll be a weapon all right on don't be  a weapon on the bad guy all right Oh in  spite of this tension Daisy loses its  edge many hours in you learn the tricks  of finding new gear and weapons and you  learn which towns to avoid in which to  ransack each respawn leaves you more  experienced in the stronger and that  confidence encourages board players to  harass newcomers and steal their  precious few valuables by the time  you've put around twenty hours you know  the secrets to crafting and making  infection prone bandages out of old  t-shirts you're a survivor with no job  but to survive in some ways that's the  appeal of the Daisy alpha since there  are no objectives and thus no hope your  only option is to keep surviving until  death inevitably overtakes you of course  this is not a finished game but Daisy is  slowly growing into the space that's  been carved out for it  but given the alphas shaky foundation  and inherent frustrations make sure to  look before you leap  you
 

